> Duplicate values do not extend strict LIS

Equal values replace the first non-smaller frontier class. For1,1, the
one-element witness comes from the later identity1. This is a deterministic
implementation tie, not an earliest-index guarantee. On1,1,2,2,3,3, the length
is3, while the nondecreasing version could retain all six values. The original
integer domain includes INT_MIN and INT_MAX; comparisons avoid subtracting
values, so no signed-value-difference overflow is needed. The following actual
strict witnesses show source identities as well as lengths.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/lis_strict.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
    std::vector<std::vector<int>> controls{{1, 1}, {1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 3},
                                           {3, 2, 1}, {1, 2, 3}, {}};
    std::vector<double> x, y;
    std::print("| explicit control index | original length | actual strict LIS length | actual selected " "source indices |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |\n");
    for (std::size_t k = 0; k < controls.size(); ++k) {
        auto a = dm::algorithms::lis_analyze(controls[k]);
        std::string indices;
        for (auto i : a.indices)
            indices += std::format("{} ", i);
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", k, controls[k].size(),
                   a.sequence.size(), indices.empty() ? "empty" : indices);
        x.push_back(k);
        y.push_back(a.sequence.size());
    }
    auto p = dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    p.points(x, y, "actual strict length", {37, 99, 235}, 6).y_limits(-.2, 4.5).title("Actual strict duplicate and order controls").x_label("listed original control index").y_label("actual strictly increasing subsequence length").save_svg("build/lis_strict.svg");
}
```
